      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "373d68d0-1a57-57fb-a475-264ee6e382e8",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Bulbophyllum, Bismarckia, Bismarckia nobilis\n- People: Otto von Bismarck, Jaap J. Vermeulen, Pfitzer, Schlechter, Mansfeld, Dressler, Dodson, Garay\n- Concepts: Orchidaceae taxonomy, botanical naming\n\nFacts:\n- In 1887, Pfitzer was one of the next great contributors to the taxonomy of the Orchidaceae.\n- In 1926, Schlechter was one of the next great contributors to the taxonomy of the Orchidaceae.\n- In 1937, Mansfeld was one of the next great contributors to the taxonomy of the Orchidaceae.\n- In 1960, Dressler and Dodson were among the next great contributors to the taxonomy of the Orchidaceae.\n- In 1960 and 1972, Garay was one of the next great contributors to the taxonomy of the Orchidaceae.\n- In 1966, Vermeulen was one of the next great contributors to the taxonomy of the Orchidaceae.\n- In 1981, Dressler was again one of the next great contributors to the taxonomy of the Orchidaceae.\n- Jaap J. Vermeulen, born in 1955, is a Dutch botanist specializing in the Orchidaceae genus Bulbophyllum.\n- Bulbophyllum is the largest genus in the orchid family Orchidaceae.\n- Bulbophyllum has more than 2,000 species and is one of the largest genera of flowering plants, exceeded only by Astragalus.\n- The genus Bismarckia was named for Otto von Bismarck, the first chancellor of the German Empire.\n- The species epithet of Bismarckia nobilis comes from Latin for 'noble'.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "98a94ed7-dbe2-5e80-8c9a-81e348e800b1",
        "text": "Page: Bulbophyllum. Sentence: Bulbophyllum is the largest genus in the orchid family Orchidaceae.\nPage: Bulbophyllum. Sentence: With more than 2,000 species, it is also one of the largest genera of flowering plants, exceeded only by \"Astragalus\".\nPage: Bismarckia. Sentence: The genus is named for the first chancellor of the German Empire Otto von Bismarck and the epithet for its only species, Bismarckia nobilis, comes from Latin for 'noble'.\nPage: Jaap J. Vermeulen. Sentence: Jaap J. Vermeulen (born 1955) is a Dutch Botanist, specializing in the Orchidaceae Genus Bulbophyllum\nPage: Taxonomy of the Orchidaceae. Sentence: The next great contributors were Pfitzer (1887), Schlechter (1926), Mansfeld (1937), Dressler and Dodson (1960), Garay (1960, 1972), Vermeulen (1966), again Dressler (1981).",
